from livestock production, with enteric fermentation as the dominant source contributing approximately 2.8 Gt CO2-eq, followed by manure management and manure left on pastures (1.2 Gt CO2-eq) (Symeon et al. 2025). The rising global population will intensify meat demand and organic waste generation, amplifying GHG emissions (IISD 2020; Wang et al. 2021). Sub-Saharan Africa generates over 62 million tons of organic waste annually, much of which is mismanaged through open dumping and uncontrolled decomposition (Orhorhoro & Oghoghorie 2019). This challenge is more pronounced as rapid urbanization and expanding livestock production in Kenya are expected to markedly increase organic waste by 2030, emphasizing the need for sustainable waste valorization solutions (National Environment Management Authority 2021). Poor organic waste disposal, often through open dumping or uncontrolled storage, results in the release of greenhouse gases (GHG) such as methane (CH4), CO2, and nitrous oxide (N2O), which have high global warming potential (GWP) and contribute significantly to climate change. These emissions are particularly pronounced in livestock and human wastes due to their high organic matter and nitrogen content, which promote microbial decomposition and gas formation under anaerobic or poorly managed conditions (Amedh 2024). Globally, livestock production generates large quantities of manure, estimated at over 4 billion tonnes per year. In many regions, including developing countries, these wastes are commonly managed through open dumping, logoon storage, or rudimentary composting systems, which can lead to substantial GHG emissions and environmental pollution (Lema 2025). Livestock wastes such as pig manure (PM) and chicken manure (CM), together with human faecal matter (FM), are particularly important emission sources during storage, handling, and treatment. PM typically contains high moisture content and readily degradable organic compounds, creating anaerobic conditions that favour methane production. CM, on the other hand, contains high nitrogen concentrations that promote nitrous oxide emissions during decomposition. Similarly, untreated FM can produce both CH4 and N2O due to microbial breakdown of organic matter and nitrogen compounds. Composting of livestock manure, although widely used as a management strategy, can still contribute significantly to GHG emissions, accounting for approximately 45–91% of total emissions from manure treatment systems, largely due to release (Nordahl et al. 2023), while untreated human FM remains a significant source of CH4 and N2O emissions (Lambiasi et al. 2024). These characteristics highlight the need for alternative waste management approaches that reduce emissions while enabling resource recovery. Black soldier fly larvae (BSFL, Hermetia illucens) bioconversion has emerged as a promising strategy for transforming organic waste into valuable products while simultaneously reducing GHG and environmental pollution. Unlike anaerobic digestion and composting, which can generate substantial CH4 and N2O emissions and require controlled infrastructure (Aguirre-Villegas & Larson 2017), BSFL treatment operates with lower energy requirements and offers rapid waste conversion with nutrient recovery. However, most existing studies have primarily focused on waste reduction efficiency, larval biomass production, and nutrient recycling, with limited comparative evaluation of greenhouse gas (GHG) dynamics and overall GWP across different waste substrates. In particular, comprehensive assessments of GWP during BSFL bioconversion of FM, PM, and CM remain insufficiently explored (Xiang et al. 2024b). Furthermore, previous studies investigating GHG emissions during waste treatment have largely relied on advanced analytical techniques such as gas chromatography-flame ionization detection (GC-FID), infrared gas analyzers, and differential optical absorption spectroscopy (Lambert-Girard et al. 2015; Shellie & Heng 2023). Although these techniques provide high analytical accuracy, their application is often constrained by high operational costs, complex maintenance requirements, and reliance on laboratory-based infrastructure. This limits their suitability for continuous monitoring in smallscale or decentralized BSFL treatment systems (Boakye-Yiadom et al. 2022). Therefore, this study integrates real-time gas sensing using (MQ-4 and MQ-135 sensors with life cycle assessment (LCA) to quantify and compare CO2, CH4, N2O, and NH3 emissions from human FM, PM, and CM during BSFL bioconversion. By evaluating the relative GWP of these substrates, the study provides insights into the environmental performance of BSFLbased waste treatment systems. GWP expresses the warming effect of different greenhouse gases relative to the emission of 1 tonne of CO2, thereby providing a standardized metric for comparing the climate impact of different waste management pathways (Shanbhag et al. 2024).
