Sampling and preparation
Fresh human FM was collected from a urine diversion dehydration toilet (UDDT) at Gachororo Primary School, Juja, Kenya, while pig and CM were collected from a private farmer in Juja, Kenya. Non-degradable materials were removed, and the substrates were mixed to obtain homogeneous samples. Samples were collected, dried at 104 ± 1 °C, and then stored in air-tight containers for elemental analysis. The dried samples were later shipped to Aarhus University in Denmark, Department of Biological and Chemical Engineering, for elemental analysis using vario MACRO cube CHNS elemental analyzer (Elementar Analysensysteme GmbH, Langenselbold, Germany) according to the procedure described by Muhammad Firdaus et al. (2018). The elements (carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and sulphur) were expressed as a percentage of the total mass of the original sample. Ash content was determined based on an oven dry weight (ODW) basis, which presents the mass of a sample after complete removal of moisture through oven drying as described in Equation (1) (Feng et al. 2005).
