Sensor calibration was performed automatically using algorithms, with calibration emissions recorded every 3 min. The control layer transferred real-time data to ‘Thinkspeak’, an open-source internet platform that receives, processes, and stores the data. The gas concentration was measured at 30 min interval for 15 days. These sensors were selected due to their affordability, accessibility, and suitability for detecting emission trends in resource-limited research contexts. However, the MQ sensor series is not a laboratory-grade instrument and therefore has limitations in precision, cross-sensitivity, and susceptibility to environmental conditions. These limitations were mitigated through calibration and repeated calibration and trials. The results were interpreted as indicative of concentration trends rather than absolute values. While sensor uncertainty may have influenced the magnitude of calculated emissions, its impact on the estimation of GWP was proportionally consistent across substrates, as all treatments were monitored under the same conditions.
Bioconversion experimental setup
The experiment followed a randomized design in a greenhouse at Jomo Kenyatta University of Agriculture and Technology (JKUAT), Nairobi, Kenya, with three homogenous mixed substrates (FM, PM, and CM). Each substrate was tested with and without BSFL (control), each in triplicate. The larvae were collected from a colony on day 6 and then starved for 24 h before being introduced to the substrates. The starvation period ensured that their guts were emptied, minimizing the risk of carryover effects from previous feeds that could otherwise bias experimental results (Shishkov et al. 2019). Six-day-old BSFL were sampled by passing them through a 1.2 mm mesh screen (Nyakeri et al. 2019). Each treatment unit consisted of 250 pieces of 7-day-old larvae, which were fed on 0.25 kg of each substrate according to a procedure outlined by Vodounnou et al. (2024). Calibrated sensors were then installed directly inside the ventilated substrate container (115 × 115 × 140 mm) in each treatment unit. DHT22 sensors were used to measure the temperature range (25.94–29.76 °C) and relative humidity level (42.9366.72%) within the greenhouse.
LCA for BSFL during the bioconversion process
This study used the cradle-to-gate approach, which provides a holistic view of BSFL environmental impacts throughout its entire life cycle. The life cycle inventory (LCI) considered inputs (FM, PM, CM water, chick mash, BSFL eggs, and BSFL), processes (larval rearing and bioconversion), outputs (gaseous emissions (CH4, CO2, N2O, and NH3) and frass), as shown in Figure 1. Infrastructure construction, chick mash production, and downstream frass utilization were excluded due to negligible contribution to system emissions. Foreground data were directly obtained from experimental measurements of gas emissions (CH4, CO2, N2O, NH3), while background data were sourced from the ecoinvent database in OpenLCA LCIA methods 2.4.0. The functional unit was the treatment of 0.25 kg of each substrate. The GWP associated with each treatment was subsequently quantified using OpenLCA, an open-source LCA software.
System boundary
The cumulative gas concentration of CH4, CO2, N2O, and NH3 measured in ppm was converted to mg/kg as per Equation (5) as described by Oyatokun (2023) under standard temperature and pressure conditions (25 °C, 1 atm) to standardize gas concentration across treatments. This conversion assumes ideal gas behaviour, serving as a comparative indicator of emission levels rather than absolute flux.
