RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Temperature and humidity recorded
The recorded temperature exhibited relatively small fluctuations, with a mean of 27.75 ± 2 °C throughout a 15-day period. In contrast, relative humidity displayed more pronounced variability, ranging between 60.72 ± 4%, as shown in Figure 2. The recorded temperature (27.75 ± 2 °C) and relative humidity (60.72 ± 4%) fall within the optimal range reported for BSFL development (25–30 °C and 60–70% RH), supporting active larval metabolism and microbial degradation. These conditions are considered favourable for efficient substrate conversion and consistent emission monitoring. Therefore, the environmental conditions during the experiment were suitable for effective bioconversion. These findings highlight the critical role of environmental conditions in regulating BSFL bioconversion and GHG emissions, suggesting that future studies should investigate these effects under controlled environments.
Characteristics of the FM, CM, and PM
Results for the elemental analysis of FM, CM, and PM are presented in Table 2. Human FM had the highest carbon (43.57%) and nitrogen (5.12%) content, yielding a relatively balanced C/N ratio of (14.65 ± 0.11%), which supported both microbial decomposition and efficient larval assimilation. This balance facilitated stable nitrification and denitrification processes, contributing to higher CH4 and N2O emissions but also sustaining larval growth through sufficient nutrient availability. PM and CM had carbon content of (28.19 and 24.52%) and lower C/N ratios (11.68 and 8.51%), indicating nitrogen-rich substrates prone to NH3 volatilization, especially in CM with its lowest C/N ratio (Zhou 2016). High ash and oxygen contents in CM (32.31 and 29.10%) further reduced its organic fraction available for larval metabolism, limiting nutrient assimilation and increasing gaseous emissions. FM provided a more
