FM released moderate concentrations of CH4 (2,205.32 ± 160.00) and N2O (3,619.55 ± 100.00), indicating active anaerobic degradation and substantial nitrogen mineralization. In contrast, PM had the lowest emissions across all gases, with CO2 (1,176.20 ± 1.70) being the most emitted and N2O (0.55 ± 0.01) the least emitted, suggesting reduced microbial activity and lower availability of degradable organic substrates. Overall, these differences highlight that the type and magnitude of gaseous emissions during decomposition are determined by substrate characteristics, including organic matter quality, carbon content, and C/N ratio, which in turn regulate microbial processes such as methanogenesis, nitrification, and denitrification. Similarly, a study reported high cumulative GHG emissions during composting from food waste and CM, where CO2 was 186,127 ppm and 173,900 ppm, respectively, highlighting significant emissions of CO2, CH4, and N2O from different substrates tested (Zeng et al. 2023). The BSFL-treated substrates recorded the lowest emissions across all the substrates tested. For instance, BSF_CM emitted 393.57 ± 1.50 ppm CH4 and 0.83 ± 0.01 ppm N2O, substantially lower than untreated CM. Similarly, BSF_FM and BSF_PM showed drastic CO2, CH4, NH3, and N2O emissions compared to FM and PM, respectively, as shown in Table 3. The substantially lower emissions from BSFL-treated substrates indicate that the larvae rapidly consume and stabilize the organic matter, reducing the availability of carbon and nitrogen for microbial processes such as methanogenesis, nitrification, and denitrification. Lindberg et al. (2022) confirmed that during composting of fruit and vegetable waste, with or without pre-treatment, CH4 and N2O emissions were very low (40–1,570 ppm) compared to CO2 (47,000–147,000 ppm). A study carried out by Xiang et al. (2024a) showed the same trend as the GHG emissions CH4, CO2, N2O, and NH3 were reduced by the larval bioreactor by 62, 87, and 95%, respectively, compared to composting. BSFL treatment significantly mitigated the emissions of CH4, CO2, N2O, and NH3. The reduction is mainly linked to improved aeration within the substrate and the larval conversion of organic carbon and nitrogen biomass, which limits the microbial pathway responsible for GHG generation. Additionally, BSFL secretes antimicrobial compounds and enzymes that alter the microbial community, suppressing methanogens and denitrifying bacteria responsible for CH4 and N2O generation. This combination of aeration, nutrient assimilation, and microbial stabilization of organic matter lowers overall emissions (Li et al. 2025). The results demonstrated that BSFL bioconversion significantly reduced GHG emissions from FM, PM, and CM, highlighting its potential as a climate mitigation strategy. CH4 and N2O were particularly reduced, while CO2 and NH3 emissions also decreased substantially, further supporting the role of BSFL in enhancing environmental sustainability.
Gas emissions from the bioconversion of the three substrates
Methane emissions from the three substrates
Among the control group, PM showed the lowest cumulative CH4 emissions, while CM showed the highest emissions. Among the BSFL-treated substrates, BSF_CM recorded the highest cumulative CH4 emissions, while BSF_PM had the lowest emissions, as shown in Figure 3. Methane emission peaked during the first few days and declined towards the end, reflecting substrate depletion and reduced methanogenic activity. The emissions were highest in untreated CM (3,544.37 ppm), FM (2,205.32 ppm), and PM (148.58 ppm), and were substantially reduced by BSFL treatment to 393.57, 195.75, and 20.30 ppm, respectively, corresponding to reduction rates of 88.90% in CM, 91.13% in FM, and 86.34% in PM, with CM showing the highest mitigation efficiency relative to its initial emission levels. The low C/N ratio of 8:51 in CM promoted rapid microbial degradation of organic matter, enhancing anaerobic conditions that support methane production. As a result, the combined effect of high ammonia levels and a low C/N ratio significantly boosted methane generation in CM, even under BSFL treatment. BSFL treatment enhanced oxygen diffusion and substrate mixing, promoting an aerobic microbial pathway that converts organic carbon to CO2 instead of CH4. This shift suppresses methanogenic processes, resulting in lower methane emissions. Ermolaev et al. (2019) observed that BSFL feeding reduces the presence of readily degradable carbon compounds, thereby limiting methanogenic precursors within the substrate. Matos et al. (2021) further noted that BSFL digestion converts a portion of organic carbon into larval biomass, thereby reducing the carbon pool available for microbial methane production. Moreover, Symeon et al. (2025) emphasized that BSFL activity shifts microbial community composition, suppressing methanogens while enhancing aerobic and facultative microbial populations. These studies predominantly used closed chamber systems with gas chromatography to quantify CH4 concentrations, ensuring precise and comparative assessment of gas emission during bioconversion. Collectively, all these studies confirm that BSFL bioconversion effectively mitigates methane generation through biochemical stabilization and microbial regulation.
