CO2 emissions from the three substrates
The cumulative CO2 concentrations measured over the 15 days are presented in Figure 4. Initially, CO2 emissions were low in both treatments during the first few days. However, emissions increased steadily as microbial activity increased, decomposing organic matter in all substrates. Emissions were highest in CM (94,853.59 ppm), followed by FM (10,210.99 ppm) and PM (1,176.20 ppm). BSFL treatment reduced these emissions to (138.22 ppm), (195.38 ppm), and (126.14 ppm), with reduction rates of 99.85, 98.09, and 89.27%, respectively. The emissions were primarily influenced by the carbon content and C/N ratio of the substrate. CM had the highest cumulative CO2 concentration among the control substrates, followed by FM and PM. FM with high carbon (43.57%) and a balanced C/N ratio (14.65%) supported elevated microbial respiration, whereas CM, despite its lower carbon (24.52%), contained a high proportion of readily degradable organic matter and a low C/N ratio (8.51), which accelerated microbial metabolism and led to high CO2 release. However, the CO2 levels recorded for the treated substrate with BSFL suggest that the use of BSFL could contribute to better atmospheric CO2 regulation and potentially offer an eco-friendly solution in reducing the GHG emissions associated with climate change impact. BSFL-treated FM generated minimal CO2 compared to extremely high levels from untreated FM, with one-way ANOVA confirming a difference of (P = 7.31 × 10−7), far below the 0.05 significance threshold, indicating BSFL treatment effectively
