under various conditions, untreated manure exhibited high emissions, which were comparable with those recorded in this study, which recorded high emissions in FM. The p-value of 5.76 × 10−7 indicates a highly significant reduction in NH3 emissions observed in BSFL-treated substrate, demonstrating the effectiveness of larvae in mitigating ammonia release during bioconversion. Similarly, in PM, a p-value of 5.70 × 10−8 further supports a significant reduction of NH3 emissions under BSFL treatment. The reduction across BSFL-treated substrates results from the larvae’s capacity to assimilate nitrogen into body protein during bioconversion, thereby limiting the nitrogen available for volatilization as ammonia. This process improves nitrogen retention and produces nutrient-rich frass suitable for use as organic fertilizer. Previous studies (Chen et al. 2019; Lopes et al. 2022; Parodi et al. 2022) further showed that BSFL bioconversion promotes nitrogen stabilization and reduces NH3 losses through biological transformation into stable organic forms, supporting the effectiveness of BSFL in mitigating nitrogen-related emissions during waste treatment. Overall, these results reaffirm that BSFL bioconversion offers a dual advantage by minimizing nitrogen-related gaseous emissions while enhancing nutrient recovery, making it a promising approach for sustainable waste management.
Nitrous oxide emissions on the three substrates
The N2O emissions from the tested substrates are presented in Figure 6. The N2O emissions varied significantly across the substrates. All BSFL treatments resulted in a decline in cumulative N2O emissions compared to the control groups. The most significant reduction occurred in CM, where emissions dropped from 44,294.91 ± 200.00 ppm to 0.83 ± 0.01 ppm, representing over a 99% decrease. FM recorded a sharp decline from 3,619.55 ± 100.00 ppm to 0.10 ± 0.11 ppm, while PM exhibited relatively low emissions, decreased from 0.55 ± 0.01 ppm to 0.16 ± 0.60 ppm. The statistically significant p-values (2.56 × 10−6, 3.84 × 10−9, and 4.11 × 10−7) further confirmed reduced N2O emissions across all BSFL-treated substrates. The observed trend results from the larvae’s capacity to assimilate nitrogen into their biomass, thereby reducing the pool of inorganic nitrogen available for nitrification and denitrifying the principal microbial pathway leading to N2O. The relatively low N2O emissions can be linked to the elemental composition of the substrates. FM, with the highest nitrogen content (5.12%), recorded slightly higher N2O concentration, while overall emissions remained low due to limited available nitrogen. However, BSFL treatment minimized N2O release by altering substrate conditions that typically promoted denitrification. The larval activity enhanced oxygen diffusion and anaerobic microsites, limiting the conversion of nitrate to N2O. Additionally, nitrogen was immobilized in stable ammonium forms, thereby restricting substrate available for microbial denitrification (Pang et al. 2020). As a result, nitrogen gases are minimized while the remaining frass retains a higher nutrient value. These mechanisms collectively explain the lower N2O emissions and highlight BSFL bioconversion as an efficient nitrogen stabilizing process (Mertenat et al. 2019; Zhang et al. 2024). These outcomes position BSFL treatment as a sustainable waste strategy that simultaneously mitigates emissions and enhances nutrient recovery, contributing to circular bioeconomy goals. Among the substrates, BSFL treatment achieved the highest CH4 mitigation in FM (91.13%), while CO2 and N2O reductions were greatest in CM (99.85 and 99%, respectively); similarly, ammonia showed its highest reduction in FM (98.89%). Overall, these results demonstrate the mitigation capacity of BSFL bioconversion, with its effectiveness varying across gases and substrates depending on the waste characteristics and microbial dynamics. To further quantify the climate implications of these emissions reductions, an LCA was conducted to translate gaseous concentrations into equivalent GWP values.
LCA for BSFL from the bioconversion process
This study focused on GWP, measured in kg CO2-equivalent, to assess which substrates, FM, PM, and CM, contributed the most to GHG emissions during the bioconversion process. A summary of the computations is presented in Table 4. The GWP of each gas determined using openLCA provided insight into the potential contribution of CH4, CO2, N2O, and NH3 emissions from each substrate to climate change, as illustrated in Figure 7. The GWP results demonstrated that BSFL has significant potential to mitigate GHG emissions, achieving reductions of 94.3% in CM, 91.7% in FM, and 80% in PM. The substrate with the highest GWP cumulatively over the 15 days was CM with emissions of (0.194 kg CO2-eq), followed by FM (0.072 kg CO2-e) and PM (0.005 kg CO2-eq). The high GWP from CM and FM resulted from rapid microbial degradation and intensive organic matter mineralization, which enhanced
