CONCLUSION
This study quantified GHG emissions from CM, PM, and human FM, and evaluated the mitigation potential of BSFL bioconversion. The results clearly demonstrated that untreated substrates emitted substantially higher concentrations of methane (CH4), CO2, nitrous oxide (N2O), and ammonia (NH3) compared to substrates treated with BSFL. Among the untreated substrates, CM produced the highest cumulative emissions and GWP, followed by human FM and PM. BSFL treatment significantly reduced GHG emissions across all substrates. The corresponding reduction in GWP was 94.3% for CM, 91.7% for human FM, and 80% for PM. The highest mitigation efficiency was observed in CM, indicating that nitrogen-rich substrates with low carbon-to-nitrogen ratios benefit most from larval bioconversion. These findings confirm that substrate composition strongly influences emission dynamics and the overall climate mitigation performance of BSFL treatment. The study addressed the key research question of whether BSFL bioconversion can effectively reduce GHG emissions from different organic waste substrates. The results provide clear evidence that larval activity reduces emissions by enhancing substrate aeration, assimilating carbon and nitrogen into biomass, and suppressing microbial pathways responsible for methane and nitrous oxide production. Although conducted under controlled laboratory conditions, the findings provide a strong experimental basis for considering BSFL bioconversion as a low-emission alternative to conventional manure management practices. Future research should focus on validating these results under field-scale conditions and assessing long-term operational performance. Overall, BSFL bioconversion demonstrates significant potential as a sustainable strategy for reducing GHG emissions from organic waste streams.
